// Pockets that weaken instead of dying, and fighters among their own people
// (Scripts/Simulation/StrandedGarrisons.cs, LogisticsCore.cs, Attrition.cs, Partisans.cs).
//
// The owner, 16 November 2026: "Divisions in pockets should weaken, but not die by themselves -
// they should die exactly when their morale is driven down to 0%. And they should still have
// some supply, which runs out." And: "Partisans work strangely - they die right where they rose."
// SimBench 730 --pockets, seeds 20260830 / 4242, before: 35 / 20 formations starved in a pocket
// nobody ever attacked, an untouched pocket lived 31 / 43 days, and four in five of the rising
// formations that were ever cut off were cut off on ground their own side held.
// docs/POCKETS_SUPPLY_2026_11_16_RU.md has the before and after.
{
  // --- a pocket goes hungry down to a floor, and stops there ---
  // The strength hunger leaves a formation with. Above PocketResistanceMinStrength (0.25), so a
  // starved pocket may still fight to the end, and far above DivisionBrokenStrength (0.06),
  // where a formation is buried. With EncircledStrengthLoss at 1 % a day (00_combat.json) and
  // its acceleration, a full division is on this floor about a month after its stores ran out.
  "PocketHungerStrengthFloor": 0.35,
  // The organisation ("morale") it settles at: low, not nothing. It was the rout threshold,
  // 0.055. An assault takes 5-13 % a day, so a starved pocket holds one to three days of one.
  "PocketHungerOrganisationFloor": 0.25,
  // The supply it settles at: what is found, bartered and taken from the dead. It was nothing,
  // which is no fighting power at all. Under the 0.20 an assault needs: it defends, it cannot attack.
  "PocketHungerSupplyFloor": 0.15,
  // A cut-off formation rests at this share of an ordinary day's recovery: half while its stores
  // last, a quarter once they are gone, and never above the ceiling its hungry days have set.
  // Both were nothing, so a pocket that beat off an assault stayed as spent as it was left.
  "PocketRecoveryWithStores": 0.5,
  "PocketRecoveryHungry": 0.25,
  // Extra men a pocket on its floor loses each week to desertion, with no floor under it: from
  // the hunger floor to nothing takes about two years. The insurance against a pocket nobody
  // attacks standing on the map for ever - not the way a pocket is beaten.
  "PocketHungerDesertionWeekly": 0.015,
  // The daily chance a hungry pocket with no relief coming gives up of its own accord. Nothing:
  // a roll that removes a formation nobody attacked is the pocket "dying by itself". The rule
  // stays for mods, and for a negotiated surrender to be built on. It was 0.12.
  "PocketSurrenderDailyChance": 0.0,

  // --- the land feeds its own (Partisans.LiveOffTheLand) ---
  // A formation of a rising (a breakaway, a movement in arms, a faction, a civil-war front) that
  // no road reaches is not cut off where the people around it are its own: they feed it.
  "PartisanSupplyEnabled": true,
  // How far the people of the region must stand behind it, 0 to 1, for this rule and the next.
  "PartisanSupportFloor": 0.35,
  // What a region of the rising's own homeland always gives, whoever lives there now.
  "PartisanHomelandSupport": 0.6,
  // Outside the homeland, the share that counts of what kinship or the region's anger gives:
  // at a half, the rising's own people have to be seven in ten of the region.
  "PartisanAbroadSupportShare": 0.5,
  // People of a region, at full support, who keep one division in the field. Tuned so the
  // smallest faction of 2024 (ninety thousand people) feeds the two or three formations it has
  // and one with millions feeds dozens: it binds only on a crowd on a scrap of ground.
  "PartisanPeoplePerDivision": 30000,
  // The supply they settle at when the land can feed everybody. Under the 0.75 of a garrison on
  // its stores (a depot against gardens), over the 0.5 below which men start to leave.
  "PartisanForageSupply": 0.65,
  // The share of that in a province the enemy controls: a garrison in the town, a curfew.
  "PartisanOccupiedGroundShare": 0.5,

  // --- go to ground and come back (Partisans.TryGoToGround, Partisans.TickStages) ---
  // A rising's formation beaten with nowhere to retreat, among its own people, scatters into
  // the villages instead of surrendering whole.
  "GuerrillaGoToGroundEnabled": true,
  // The share of its men who get away. The other four in ten are the price of the lost battle:
  // dead (SurrenderKilledShare) and prisoners. One detachment comes back about twice.
  "GuerrillaGoToGroundShare": 0.6,
  // Each week, the chance a region's hidden fighters field a detachment: about a month between
  // going to ground and coming back.
  "GuerrillaReformWeeklyChance": 0.25,
  // The least they field, as a share of a militia formation (common/militia, 9000 men).
  "GuerrillaReformMinShare": 0.5,
  // The organisation a detachment comes back with. A fresh militia has 0.45; these mustered in a hurry.
  "GuerrillaReformOrganisation": 0.35,
  // The share of the hidden who go back to their lives each week: 27,000 are under one
  // detachment in sixty weeks, 9,000 in twenty-three. The time an occupier pays for in garrisons.
  "GuerrillaBankDecayWeekly": 0.03
}
